1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
What do french fries, TV dinners,

2
00:00:03,001 --> 00:00:06,000
and bottles of baby formula have in common?

3
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Nothing, right?

4
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:09,000
But at one point, they were all considered

5
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:12,000
to be the future of food.

6
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:14,000
Americans have always had a knack for inventing

7
00:00:14,001 --> 00:00:16,000
and reinventing the future.

8
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:19,000
We have a long history of pushing food technology

9
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:24,000
to create new flavors, new textures, and new foods.

10
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:27,000
Like the surprising origins of Cheetos,

11
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:29,000
launching ice cream into outer space,

12
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:32,000
and adding eggs to a box of Betty Crocker powder

13
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:35,440
to, poof, bake a magical cake.

14
00:00:35,540 --> 00:00:38,840
Today, we'll get a sneak peek of the delicious innovations

15
00:00:38,940 --> 00:00:41,740
in our food future, from the sci-fi way

16
00:00:41,840 --> 00:00:45,680
to grow veggies without sunlight to robots

17
00:00:45,780 --> 00:00:49,480
autonomously crafting hamburgers and pizza.

18
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:53,520
And you won't believe what scientists discovered in plants

19
00:00:53,620 --> 00:00:58,000
that's also found in meat and creates the delicious meat

20
00:00:58,001 --> 00:01:00,340
meat flavor that we crave.

21
00:01:00,440 --> 00:01:03,200
Come take a ride with me in my flying car,

22
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:06,740
because this is Modern Marvel's The Future of Food.

23
00:01:22,360 --> 00:01:24,600
For a glimpse into the future, I've

24
00:01:24,700 --> 00:01:27,560
come here to Natick, Massachusetts.

25
00:01:27,561 --> 00:01:30,700
Natick's General Equipment and Packaging Laboratory

26
00:01:30,701 --> 00:01:32,840
has both small items and large.

27
00:01:33,500 --> 00:01:37,420
The military's funding of food research dates back to World War II.

28
00:01:37,920 --> 00:01:41,880
And by 1963, their food research and development mission

29
00:01:41,881 --> 00:01:43,840
moved here to Natick Labs.

30
00:01:44,280 --> 00:01:46,560
These food development efforts by the military

31
00:01:46,561 --> 00:01:49,900
are how American classics like sandwich bread,

32
00:01:50,120 --> 00:01:53,840
the McRib, and some would even say Cheetos, were created.

33
00:01:53,841 --> 00:02:00,640
So to see what they do here is to see a glimpse of our future eats.

34
00:02:00,641 --> 00:02:07,120
To learn how they do it, Lori Aleksic is taking us behind the scenes of this vast compound

35
00:02:07,121 --> 00:02:09,986
of 50 buildings spanning 78 acres.

36
00:02:09,987 --> 00:02:14,960
Lori is the team leader of food engineering and analysis at

37
00:02:14,961 --> 00:02:20,383
the Army's facilities. How are you? How are you? A little bit of patriotic elbow.

38
00:02:20,384 --> 00:02:21,120
You develop

39
00:02:21,121 --> 00:02:22,320
Everything here?

40
00:02:22,321 --> 00:02:25,160
Yes, we're the only DOD facility

41
00:02:25,161 --> 00:02:27,000
that conducts the research and development

42
00:02:27,001 --> 00:02:28,500
of field food service equipment.

43
00:02:28,501 --> 00:02:31,700
So how often are you introducing new products?

44
00:02:31,701 --> 00:02:33,040
Every single year.

45
00:02:33,041 --> 00:02:34,360
Really? Yep.

46
00:02:34,361 --> 00:02:36,740
Lori's team are the skilled pros,

47
00:02:36,741 --> 00:02:38,980
constantly testing new food technologies

48
00:02:38,981 --> 00:02:40,980
for use by the military today,

49
00:02:40,981 --> 00:02:46,320
but soon could be the future of consumer food tomorrow.

50
00:02:46,321 --> 00:02:49,580
And speaking of the future, while I suit up,

51
00:02:49,581 --> 00:02:52,580
we're making a pit stop to a company in the Bay Area

52
00:02:52,581 --> 00:02:56,580
that delivers an old favorite with a science fiction spin.

53
00:03:02,920 --> 00:03:06,100
Creator is the first restaurant to fully automate

54
00:03:06,101 --> 00:03:08,100
a major sector of food.

55
00:03:08,101 --> 00:03:11,100
They're making hamburgers by robot.

56
00:03:16,980 --> 00:03:19,580
With the app, you can order from the ground up

57
00:03:19,581 --> 00:03:21,920
You can specify some of the usual customization things,

58
00:03:21,921 --> 00:03:24,820
like extra tomatoes, no pickles.

59
00:03:24,821 --> 00:03:27,120
You can also do things you can't do anywhere else.

60
00:03:27,121 --> 00:03:29,220
You choose every item,

61
00:03:29,221 --> 00:03:32,120
down to the milliliter of sauces and seasonings

62
00:03:32,121 --> 00:03:33,620
that make up your burger.

63
00:03:33,621 --> 00:03:35,120
Finally, when you place your order,

64
00:03:35,121 --> 00:03:37,440
the apple even tell you exactly how long it takes

65
00:03:37,441 --> 00:03:38,840
to make your burger, because guess what?

66
00:03:38,841 --> 00:03:39,940
It's all made by a machine.

67
00:03:39,941 --> 00:03:41,840
We know exactly when everything's gonna be ready.

68
00:03:41,841 --> 00:03:46,780
At full go, it can produce 150 burgers an hour.

69
00:03:46,781 --> 00:03:50,120
But efficiency is only the beginning.

70
00:03:50,220 --> 00:03:52,780
Creator's robot takes a fresh hamburger bun

71
00:03:52,880 --> 00:03:55,960
and guides it through a blade, slicing it in half.

72
00:03:56,060 --> 00:03:59,720
A series of motors then butters and moves the sliced bun

73
00:03:59,820 --> 00:04:02,600
through an open view toaster so you can actually see

74
00:04:02,700 --> 00:04:04,500
the browning as it's happening.

75
00:04:04,600 --> 00:04:07,040
A conveyor belt advances the toasted bun

76
00:04:07,140 --> 00:04:09,640
to the next position, the saucer.

77
00:04:09,740 --> 00:04:13,780
Some of their sauce varieties include smoked oyster aioli,

78
00:04:13,880 --> 00:04:16,540
their own take on a thousand island dressing,

79
00:04:16,541 --> 00:04:21,540
sunflower seed tahini, and shiitake mushroom sauce.

80
00:04:21,640 --> 00:04:25,240
Then your topping selections are all cut fresh to order

81
00:04:25,340 --> 00:04:27,480
and are carefully placed onto your bun.

82
00:04:27,580 --> 00:04:29,620
The next up is cheese.

83
00:04:29,720 --> 00:04:32,520
Your selection of cheese is freshly grated onto your bun

84
00:04:32,620 --> 00:04:34,960
and then moves down to a torch-like melter

85
00:04:35,060 --> 00:04:37,960
to give you that perfect stringy yet gooey texture

86
00:04:38,060 --> 00:04:39,960
when you bite into your burger.

87
00:04:40,060 --> 00:04:41,500
While the robot has been preparing

88
00:04:41,600 --> 00:04:43,500
all of your personalized toppings,

89
00:04:43,600 --> 00:04:46,000
it's also been grinding the beef to order.

90
00:04:46,001 --> 00:04:49,000
We throw cubes of chuck and brisket right in the machine.

91
00:04:49,100 --> 00:04:51,040
Not only does it grind it to order,

92
00:04:51,140 --> 00:04:55,340
it, after grinds it, aligns the meat to go vertically,

93
00:04:55,440 --> 00:04:58,440
along with the way your teeth bite into the patty.

94
00:04:58,540 --> 00:05:00,940
And that makes it more tenderly fall apart into your mouth.

95
00:05:01,040 --> 00:05:03,660
The freshest and most tender of burger patties

96
00:05:03,760 --> 00:05:07,060
is also custom-seasoned by you.

97
00:05:07,160 --> 00:05:10,100
You get to choose from 12 different seasonings

98
00:05:10,200 --> 00:05:12,900
and how much of each you'd like on your burger.

99
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:14,700
And with choreographed timing,

100
00:05:14,701 --> 00:05:18,140
your freshly grilled patty meets your custom dress bun

101
00:05:18,141 --> 00:05:21,320
at the end of the line where the two are assembled.

102
00:05:21,660 --> 00:05:24,020
All I did was push a button on a phone

103
00:05:24,021 --> 00:05:28,280
and made the freshest, most personalized burger out there.

104
00:05:29,780 --> 00:05:30,860
That's freaking good.

105
00:05:31,420 --> 00:05:34,620
Americans have loved a good hamburger for decades,

106
00:05:34,880 --> 00:05:38,260
but you'd be surprised to know a lot of other foods we love

107
00:05:38,261 --> 00:05:40,640
exist because of the military.

108
00:05:40,641 --> 00:05:44,040
To show how much American food has changed

109
00:05:44,041 --> 00:05:46,040
in just 50 years.

110
00:05:46,041 --> 00:05:49,740
It's vastly different, even just two generations back,

111
00:05:49,741 --> 00:05:53,140
what Americans are eating and what Americans see

112
00:05:53,141 --> 00:05:56,560
as even being an American food.

113
00:05:56,561 --> 00:05:59,460
Some of the everyday foods Americans eat today

114
00:05:59,461 --> 00:06:02,460
became everyday foods because of the military.

115
00:06:02,461 --> 00:06:06,700
Take, for example, soft and delicious sandwich bread.

116
00:06:06,701 --> 00:06:09,800
During World War II, military food scientists

117
00:06:09,801 --> 00:06:13,340
came up with a formula to keep bread soft and fresh

118
00:06:13,341 --> 00:06:16,880
even longer than before, which is still being utilized

119
00:06:16,881 --> 00:06:18,940
commercially to this day.

120
00:06:18,941 --> 00:06:22,720
Plus, Cheeto lovers might know, during World War II,

121
00:06:22,721 --> 00:06:26,720
the military funded research in dehydrating cheese,

122
00:06:26,721 --> 00:06:29,720
which eventually led to cheese powder.

123
00:06:29,721 --> 00:06:32,560
At the end of the war, the surplus of cheese powder

124
00:06:32,561 --> 00:06:35,860
was sold to food makers like the Frito Company,

125
00:06:35,861 --> 00:06:39,960
which covered corn puffs with the magical cheesy dust.

126
00:06:39,961 --> 00:06:41,940
It's the sort of thing that you just can't stop

127
00:06:41,941 --> 00:06:42,940
having more of.

128
00:06:42,941 --> 00:06:44,940
And there's just something about the satisfaction

129
00:06:44,941 --> 00:06:46,940
at the very end where you lick your fingers.

130
00:06:46,941 --> 00:06:48,940
That specifically, that last moment,

131
00:06:48,941 --> 00:06:50,940
is what I most remember growing up.

132
00:06:50,941 --> 00:06:53,940
We may not have had any of these culinary advances

133
00:06:53,941 --> 00:06:56,940
without food technologists like Lori and her team,

134
00:06:56,941 --> 00:06:59,940
who are constantly finding the newest ways

135
00:06:59,941 --> 00:07:02,940
to push food into the future.

136
00:07:02,941 --> 00:07:04,940
Today, what we'll be able to show you

137
00:07:04,941 --> 00:07:07,940
is we're doing a tube food production.

