242
00:12:12,701 --> 00:12:16,880
They're looking at meat not just as a byproduct of animals.

243
00:12:16,881 --> 00:12:20,340
They're breaking it down to inspect each and every molecule

244
00:12:20,341 --> 00:12:23,020
so they can figure out what plant-based sources

245
00:12:23,021 --> 00:12:25,180
contain the same molecules.

246
00:12:25,181 --> 00:12:29,140
Then they extract those molecules from plant products

247
00:12:29,141 --> 00:12:33,420
and mash them up together again to replicate the juicy, tender,

248
00:12:33,421 --> 00:12:37,120
meaty experience we as meat lovers crave.

249
00:12:37,121 --> 00:12:39,340
When you actually break it down and ask consumers,

250
00:12:39,341 --> 00:12:42,340
what is the number one most important thing when choosing

251
00:12:42,341 --> 00:12:44,020
the food that they purchase?

252
00:12:44,021 --> 00:12:47,300
Flavor is always their number one choice.

253
00:12:47,301 --> 00:12:49,980
So our first goal was to really understand what

254
00:12:49,981 --> 00:12:51,740
makes meat taste like meat.

255
00:12:51,741 --> 00:12:53,980
Actually, it's a really complex mixture

256
00:12:53,981 --> 00:12:56,320
of like over 200 compounds.

257
00:12:56,321 --> 00:12:59,660
Impossible Foods does this thanks to this machine

258
00:12:59,661 --> 00:13:02,620
right here, the gas chromatography mass

259
00:13:02,621 --> 00:13:05,020
spectrometry machine.

260
00:13:05,021 --> 00:13:07,820
Let's just call it the GCMS.

261
00:13:07,821 --> 00:13:10,600
This thing takes a piece of meat and breaks it down

262
00:13:10,601 --> 00:13:15,900
until it reaches the gas phase, then generates a report

263
00:13:15,901 --> 00:13:18,240
telling you what aromas and flavors

264
00:13:18,241 --> 00:13:20,340
are detected in the meat sample.

265
00:13:20,341 --> 00:13:21,340
Not just beef.

266
00:13:21,341 --> 00:13:25,680
You could put a piece of chicken, pork, or even fish in there,

267
00:13:25,681 --> 00:13:29,900
and the GCMS will tell you what smells and tastes are present.

268
00:13:29,901 --> 00:13:32,360
You can see that the detector is picking up

269
00:13:32,361 --> 00:13:34,260
all of these individual peaks, which

270
00:13:34,261 --> 00:13:37,560
represent different flavor compounds and aromas.

271
00:13:37,561 --> 00:13:40,740
So it's our job then to go in and understand

272
00:13:40,741 --> 00:13:43,580
which of those are the most important to make something

273
00:13:43,581 --> 00:13:46,000
that your brain recognizes as meat.

274
00:13:46,001 --> 00:13:48,920
But flavors and aromas don't add up to meat.

275
00:13:48,921 --> 00:13:50,340
Where's the beef?

276
00:13:50,341 --> 00:13:52,000
Well, that's what comes next.

277
00:13:52,001 --> 00:13:55,100
After the GCMS showed what compounds were present,

278
00:13:55,101 --> 00:13:58,000
the crazy smart scientists at Impossible Foods

279
00:13:58,001 --> 00:14:01,400
discovered something present across all living matter,

280
00:14:01,401 --> 00:14:02,940
including plants.

281
00:14:03,860 --> 00:14:06,640
They discovered this iron molecule called heme,

282
00:14:06,641 --> 00:14:10,840
which is the key ingredient that makes meat taste like meat.

283
00:14:10,841 --> 00:14:13,660
And when people crave meat,

284
00:14:13,661 --> 00:14:15,620
it's really a craving for heme.

285
00:14:15,621 --> 00:14:18,140
Heme is the iron-containing molecule

286
00:14:18,141 --> 00:14:22,180
that aids in carrying oxygen throughout all living matter,

287
00:14:22,181 --> 00:14:24,320
animals and plants.

288
00:14:24,321 --> 00:14:26,880
It is essential for life.

289
00:14:26,881 --> 00:14:32,300
And it turns out heme is what gives meat that flavor we crave.

290
00:14:32,301 --> 00:14:34,760
If you look at different types of meat,

291
00:14:34,761 --> 00:14:36,060
the only thing that's different is really

292
00:14:36,061 --> 00:14:37,800
the concentration of heme.

293
00:14:37,801 --> 00:14:41,520
So red meat and beef from cows is red

294
00:14:41,521 --> 00:14:43,220
because it has a lot of heme.

295
00:14:43,221 --> 00:14:46,340
And pork is a little less red because it has less heme.

296
00:14:46,341 --> 00:14:47,960
And then chicken, even less so.

297
00:14:47,961 --> 00:14:50,860
It also is what's giving you that color

298
00:14:50,861 --> 00:14:53,500
and actually goes through the same exact color transition

299
00:14:53,501 --> 00:14:54,500
when you cook it.

300
00:14:54,501 --> 00:14:57,680
So once the scientists came across heme,

301
00:14:57,681 --> 00:15:00,560
they got to working on how to sustainably and rapidly

302
00:15:00,561 --> 00:15:02,680
reproduce the molecule in bulk.

303
00:15:02,681 --> 00:15:06,060
And for the science geniuses at Impossible, it was simple.

304
00:15:06,061 --> 00:15:07,880
Yeast fermentation.

305
00:15:07,881 --> 00:15:10,700
The process that we use to ferment yeast to make heme

306
00:15:10,701 --> 00:15:13,680
is used to make beer and wine.

307
00:15:13,681 --> 00:15:16,960
And we're utilizing that same process of fermentation

308
00:15:16,961 --> 00:15:20,060
from yeast in order to make our key ingredient.

309
00:15:20,061 --> 00:15:22,300
And for all of you doubters out there,

